.TH NABD 1 "2026-11-02" "nabd 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
nabd \- a doctor for Linux servers: finds problems, explains them and gives the fix
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B nabd
.RB [ check ]
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B nabd why
.I topic
.RI [ arg ]
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B nabd changes
.RB [ \-\-since
.IR when ]
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B nabd snapshot
.RB [ \-\-keep
.IR n ]
.RB [ \-\-list ]
.RB [ \-\-dir
.IR dir ]
.br
.B nabd diff
.RI [ old " [" new ]]
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B nabd checks
|
.B nabd version
|
.B nabd help
.RI [ command ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B nabd
checks a Linux machine in about a second: disk space and inodes, memory, CPU,
failed services, the kernel log, time synchronisation, pending reboots,
package state, network and DNS, open ports, SSH settings, TLS certificates,
processes and accounts.
For each problem it says what is wrong and why, in plain words, and prints the
command that fixes it.
.PP
.B nabd
only reads.
It never writes, deletes, restarts or installs anything.
.B nabd snapshot
writes only its own snapshot files, and
.B \-o
writes only the file that you name.
It sends nothing over the network; only
.B nabd why dns
sends DNS queries.
.PP
.B nabd
works without root and tells you what it could not read;
.B sudo nabd
sees everything.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BR check " (the default)"
Run all checks.
Problems come first, worst first, each with
.IR why ,
.I fix
and sometimes a
.IR note .
Lines of a fix that start with
.B #
are advice, the others are commands.
.TP
.BI "why " topic " \fR[\fIarg\fR]"
Explain one question in depth.
The topics are:
.RS
.TP
.BR disk " [\fIpath\fR]"
What fills a file system: the biggest folders and files, logs, caches,
container images and deleted files that a process still keeps open.
Without a path, the fullest file system.
.TP
.B slow
What slows the machine now: CPU, memory, the disk, a network or FUSE file
system, or the hypervisor (steal), and which process.
.TP
.B memory
Who uses the memory, OOM kills, swap and tmpfs.
.TP
.BI "service " name
Why a service stopped or misbehaves: what the exit code means, the result of
its config test, the files and packages that changed before, and the logs.
.TP
.BI "port " n
Who listens on the port, from which package, which config sets it, who can
connect, and the firewall.
.TP
.BR dns " [\fIname\fR]"
Asks every DNS server for the name (default: example.com) and shows which one
fails.
This sends DNS queries.
.TP
.B boot
How the last boot went: slow units, failures, and whether the previous
shutdown was clean.
.RE
.IP
Short forms: a number is a port
.RB ( "nabd why 443" ),
a path is a disk
.RB ( "nabd why /var" ),
any other word is a service
.RB ( "nabd why nginx" ).
.TP
.B changes
A timeline of what changed: packages, files in
.IR /etc ,
SSH keys, cron tables, logins, boots and failed services.
It reads logs and file times and needs no earlier snapshot.
Changes that touch security are marked with
.BR ! .
.TP
.B snapshot
Save the security state of the machine: users, members of admin groups,
SUID and SGID files, open ports, enabled services, packages, the firewall and
SELinux state, kernel settings, and a SHA-256 hash of every file in
.IR /etc ,
of SSH
.I authorized_keys
files and of cron tables.
After saving, it prints how the state differs from the previous snapshot.
.TP
.BR diff " [\fIold\fR [\fInew\fR]]"
Compare two states.
.I old
and
.I new
are snapshot files or names, or one of
.BR last ,
.BR prev ,
.B first
and
.BR now .
Without arguments: the last snapshot and now.
.TP
.B checks
List the checks.
.TP
.B version
Print the version.
.SH OPTIONS
Options can be written before or after the command.
.SS Output
.TP
.B \-\-json
JSON for scripts (schema
.BR nabd/1 ).
.TP
.BR \-\-md ", " \-\-markdown
Markdown for a ticket or a chat.
.TP
.B \-\-nagios
Nagios plugin output: a status line with performance data, then one line per
problem.
.TP
.BR \-\-prometheus ", " \-\-prom
Metrics in the Prometheus text format, for the textfile collector of
node_exporter.
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-output " \fIfile\fR"
Write the output to
.I file
instead of standard output.
The file is replaced atomically.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Also show the checks that are OK and why a check was skipped.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Show only problems, no notes.
.TP
.BI \-\-color " when"
.BR auto " (default), " always " or " never .
.B NO_COLOR
is respected.
.B \-\-no\-color
is the same as
.BR "\-\-color never" .
.TP
.B \-\-ascii
Use plain ASCII symbols.
.SS Checks
.TP
.BI \-\-only " list"
Run only these checks (a comma separated list; see
.BR "nabd checks" ).
.TP
.BI \-\-skip " list"
Do not run these checks.
.TP
.BI \-\-timeout " duration"
The time limit for each check (default 15s), or for the scan of
.B why disk
(default 20s).
Examples: 30s, 2m.
.SS Time
.TP
.BI \-\-since " when"
For
.B changes
(default: 24h) and
.BR diff .
.I when
is a duration (90m, 24h, 3d, 1w),
.BR today ,
.BR yesterday ,
.B boot
or a date (2026\-11\-01, "2026\-11\-01 14:00").
.SS Snapshots
.TP
.BI \-\-keep " n"
Keep only the newest
.I n
snapshots.
.TP
.B \-\-list
List the saved snapshots.
.TP
.BI \-\-dir " dir"
The folder of the snapshots.
.SH EXIT STATUS
For
.B check
and
.BR why ,
the Nagios plugin rule:
.TP
.B 0
healthy (notes do not count)
.TP
.B 1
a warning
.TP
.B 2
a critical problem
.TP
.B 3
unknown: a check could not finish, or nabd could not run (for example a bad option)
.PP
For
.B changes
and
.BR diff :
0 when nothing changed, 1 when something changed, 2 for a critical difference
(diff only: a new UID 0 account, a SUID file in /tmp, /var/tmp, /dev/shm or
/home), 3 for an error.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /var/lib/nabd/snapshots
Snapshots taken as root.
.TP
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/nabd/snapshots
Snapshots of a normal user (default
.IR ~/.local/state/nabd/snapshots ).
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NABD_STATE_DIR
Use this folder for snapshots (they go to
.IR $NABD_STATE_DIR/snapshots ).
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
No colours when set.
.TP
.B COLUMNS
The width of the terminal.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B sudo nabd
Check everything.
.TP
.B nabd why disk /var
What fills /var.
.TP
.B nabd why nginx
Why nginx stopped.
.TP
.B nabd changes \-\-since boot
What changed since the machine started.
.TP
.B sudo systemctl enable \-\-now nabd\-snapshot.timer
Take a snapshot every day; later,
.B sudo nabd diff \-\-since 7d
shows what changed in a week.
.TP
.B nabd \-\-prometheus \-o /var/lib/prometheus/node\-exporter/nabd.prom
Metrics for node_exporter.
.TP
.B nabd \-\-nagios \-\-skip cpu
A Nagios plugin without the CPU check.
.SH SECURITY
.B nabd
runs only commands that read:
.BR "systemctl show" ,
.BR "systemctl list\-units" ,
.BR journalctl ,
.BR dmesg ,
.BR "timedatectl show" ,
.BR "chronyc tracking" ,
.BR "sshd \-T" ,
.BR last ,
the package database tools
.RB ( dpkg\-query ,
.BR rpm ,
.BR pacman ,
.BR apk ),
the firewall tools in list mode
.RB ( "nft list ruleset" ,
.BR "iptables \-S" ),
and the config tests of services
.RB ( "nginx \-t" ,
.BR "apachectl \-t" ,
.BR "sshd \-t" ,
.BR "haproxy \-c" ,
and others).
Every command runs with
.BR LC_ALL=C ,
a time limit and a limit on its output.
.PP
The output can contain host details such as user names, ports and paths.
Read it before you share it.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR df (1),
.BR du (1),
.BR free (1),
.BR systemctl (1),
.BR journalctl (1),
.BR ss (8)
.PP
https://github.com/s0sta/nabd
.SH BUGS
Report false alarms and missed problems at https://github.com/s0sta/nabd/issues
with the output of
.BR "nabd \-\-json" .
.SH AUTHOR
Hosam Hani (s0sta) and contributors.
